DAY 36 - PART A: THEORY
Control Plane Internals: The API Server and etcd
Time: 45 minutes
==================================================================

1. THE API SERVER REQUEST PIPELINE
----------------------------------
Every request (kubectl, kubelet, controllers, operators) passes:

  HTTPS request
    -> 1. AUTHENTICATION    who? (x509 cert, bearer token, OIDC,
                             ServiceAccount token, webhook)
    -> 2. AUTHORIZATION     allowed? (Node authorizer, RBAC, webhook)
    -> 3. MUTATING ADMISSION   may CHANGE the object
                             (defaults, LimitRange, ServiceAccount,
                             webhooks like Istio sidecar injection)
    -> 4. SCHEMA VALIDATION
    -> 5. VALIDATING ADMISSION may REJECT
                             (ResourceQuota, PodSecurity, Kyverno/OPA
                             webhooks, ValidatingAdmissionPolicy)
    -> 6. PERSIST TO etcd
    -> 7. notify WATCHERS (scheduler, controllers, kubelets)

You saw admission in action: LimitRange injected defaults (Day 29),
PodSecurity and ResourceQuota rejected pods (Days 29, 34).


2. API STRUCTURE
----------------
  /api/v1/...                       core group (pods, services ...)
  /apis/apps/v1/...                 named groups (deployments ...)
  /apis/<group>/<version>/namespaces/<ns>/<resource>/<name>

Versions: v1alpha1 (experimental) -> v1beta1 -> v1 (stable).
Watches: clients hold a long connection and receive ADDED / MODIFIED /
DELETED events. resourceVersion lets them resume. This is how all
components stay in sync without polling.


3. etcd
-------
- Distributed, consistent key-value store (Raft consensus).
- Keys like /registry/pods/default/web, values in protobuf.
- Only the API server talks to etcd (with client certificates).
- Cluster sizes: 1 (lab), 3 or 5 (production). Needs a MAJORITY
  (quorum) to accept writes: 3 nodes tolerate 1 failure, 5 tolerate 2.
- Sensitive to disk latency: use fast SSDs.
- Back it up! Losing etcd = losing the cluster's state (Day 40).


4. HOW THE CONTROL PLANE RUNS (kubeadm / kind)
----------------------------------------------
As STATIC PODS: the kubelet on the control-plane node reads YAML files
in /etc/kubernetes/manifests and runs them directly, without the API
server (it can't depend on itself).
  kube-apiserver.yaml, etcd.yaml, kube-scheduler.yaml,
  kube-controller-manager.yaml
Edit a file -> kubelet restarts that component.
In the API you see "mirror pods" (read-only reflections).


5. CERTIFICATES (PKI)
---------------------
/etc/kubernetes/pki holds the cluster CA and certs:
  ca.crt/key            cluster CA (signs apiserver, kubelet clients ...)
  apiserver.crt         API server's serving cert
  apiserver-kubelet-client.crt
  etcd/ca.crt, etcd/server.crt ...   separate etcd CA
  front-proxy-*         for API aggregation
  sa.key/sa.pub         sign ServiceAccount tokens
kubeadm certs are valid 1 year; "kubeadm certs check-expiration" and
cluster upgrades renew them.

Kubeconfig files in /etc/kubernetes:
  admin.conf, super-admin.conf, controller-manager.conf,
  scheduler.conf, kubelet.conf


6. HIGH AVAILABILITY
--------------------
Production: 3 control-plane nodes behind a load balancer;
API servers are stateless (all active); scheduler and controller
manager run active/standby via LEADER ELECTION (Lease objects in
kube-system); etcd stacked on the same nodes or external.
Managed clouds hide all of this (Week 7).


KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER
----------------------
- authn -> authz -> mutating admission -> validation -> validating
  admission -> etcd -> watchers.
- etcd needs quorum and backups.
- Control plane = static pods from /etc/kubernetes/manifests.
